Phase 3: Mando generation on shared ChatClient, judge moved off critical path

LLMGenerator migrated to the shared ChatClient. Removed the duplicated
httpx/tenacity stack, which had already drifted (0.3s/3s backoff vs the
client's rate-limit-sized 2s/30s over 5 attempts) and parsed replies with
dict.get() and no schema.

Fixed the reasoning-model truncation: max_tokens 400 -> 3000, the configuration
validated across 600 judge calls in E15. At 400 sarvam-105b spends the whole
budget on reasoning_content and returns content=None.

Structured output: strict Pydantic GenerationOutput with extra="forbid",
source-number coercion, and corrective retry on schema violation. Citation
validation drops out-of-range source numbers into Generation.invalid_sources
so fabricated citations are visible rather than swallowed.

Persona is now configurable (PersonaConfig: name, voice, enabled), Mando by
default. voice is a TTS-selection field ONLY and never enters the prompt, so
male/female can be added without touching the RAG core -- pinned by a
prompt-is-voice-invariant test. Konkani gets an explicit honesty clause telling
the model to fall back to English rather than fake fluency we cannot verify.

Judge is ASYNC by default. Its measured P50 909ms / P70 11.1s cannot sit in a
137ms budget, so:
  cache miss -> answer from the cosine guard now, judge in the background
  cache hit  -> the verdict gates at dict-lookup cost
VerdictCache keys on sha256(normalized query | sorted evidence ids | index
version); claim() collapses duplicate in-flight judgements so a burst of the
same question fires one call. judge_async_ms is tracked separately and is never
counted in total_rag_ms.

Also added: same-language enforcement (detect and warn, never refuse a correct
answer over a cosmetic mismatch), generator/persona/citation observability on
RAGResponse.

237 tests pass (was 177).

